# Important Passes, Straits, Canals, and International Boundaries

> "Which pass connects Manali and Leh?" "Which strait separates India from Sri
> Lanka?" "The Durand Line lies between which two countries?" These
> map-based facts appear in SSC, RRB and police papers year after year. The
> lists are short and fixed, so this lesson sorts them into four tables with a
> one-line hook for each.

## 1. Mountain Passes of India

[definition]
A **mountain pass** is a natural gap or saddle in a mountain range that lets
roads, rail lines and traders cross. In Tibetan and Ladakhi, **La** means
pass.
[/definition]

| Pass | Region | Connects or leads to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Zoji La** | Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh border | Srinagar with Kargil and Leh |
| **Banihal** | Pir Panjal range, J&K | Jammu with the Kashmir Valley (Jawahar Tunnel below it) |
| **Pir Panjal Pass** | Pir Panjal range | Old Mughal route into Kashmir |
| **Khardung La** | Ladakh | Leh with the Nubra Valley |
| **Rohtang** | Himachal Pradesh | Kullu (Manali) with Lahaul-Spiti; the Atal Tunnel lies beneath |
| **Baralacha La** | Himachal Pradesh | Manali-Leh highway |
| **Shipki La** | Himachal Pradesh | Sutlej enters India from Tibet; trade pass to China |
| **Lipulekh** | Uttarakhand | Kailash Mansarovar route to Tibet |
| **Niti and Mana** | Uttarakhand | Passes to Tibet in the Garhwal Himalaya |
| **Nathu La** | Sikkim | Sikkim to Tibet (China); border trade pass |
| **Jelep La** | Sikkim | Sikkim to Tibet, near Nathu La |
| **Bomdi La** | Arunachal Pradesh | Gateway to Tawang |
| **Diphu Pass** | Arunachal Pradesh | Near the India, China and Myanmar tri-junction |

- **Karakoram Pass** lies in the Ladakh region on the road to Xinjiang, China.
- **Burzil Pass** links the Kashmir Valley with Gilgit-Baltistan.
- **Shipki La** matters because the Sutlej river enters India there.

### Passes Through the Western Ghats

| Pass | Connects | State |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Palghat (Palakkad) Gap** | Kochi with Coimbatore and Chennai | Kerala and Tamil Nadu |
| **Thal Ghat** | Mumbai and Nashik | Maharashtra |
| **Bhor Ghat** | Mumbai and Pune | Maharashtra |

The Palghat Gap is a true **gap** in the Ghats, not a pass over a ridge, and
it carries the rail and road link between Kerala and Tamil Nadu.

### Passes Outside India

- **Khyber Pass**: between Pakistan and Afghanistan (Peshawar to Kabul).
- **Bolan Pass**: in Balochistan, Pakistan, an old route into South Asia.
- **Khunjerab Pass**: Pakistan to China along the Karakoram Highway.

## 2. Straits and Channels

[definition]
A **strait** is a narrow natural waterway that joins two larger water bodies
and separates two land masses. A **canal** is a man-made waterway.
[/definition]

| Strait or channel | Separates or joins |
| --- | --- |
| **Palk Strait** | India (Tamil Nadu) and Sri Lanka; joins the Bay of Bengal with Palk Bay |
| **Gulf of Mannar** | Lies south of the Palk Strait between India and Sri Lanka |
| **Ten Degree Channel** | Andaman Islands from the Nicobar Islands |
| **Eight Degree Channel** | Minicoy (India) from the Maldives |
| **Nine Degree Channel** | Minicoy from the main Lakshadweep islands |
| **Malacca Strait** | Malay Peninsula and Sumatra; joins the Andaman Sea with the South China Sea |
| **Sunda Strait** | Java and Sumatra |
| **Strait of Hormuz** | Persian Gulf with the Gulf of Oman; Iran to the north, Oman to the south |
| **Bab-el-Mandeb** | Red Sea with the Gulf of Aden; Yemen from Djibouti |
| **Bosphorus and Dardanelles** | Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara to the Aegean; both in Turkey |
| **Strait of Gibraltar** | Atlantic Ocean with the Mediterranean; Spain from Morocco |
| **Bering Strait** | Asia (Russia) from North America (Alaska) |
| **Dover Strait** | England from France, in the English Channel |
| **Taiwan Strait** | China mainland and Taiwan |
| **Mozambique Channel** | Africa from Madagascar |

- The **Palk Strait** is named after **Robert Palk**, a Governor of Madras.
- **Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu)** is a chain of shoals between Pamban Island
  (India) and Mannar Island (Sri Lanka).
- India's sea lanes to Europe pass the **Malacca Strait**, **Hormuz** and
  **Bab-el-Mandeb**, so they appear in current-affairs questions.

## 3. Famous Canals

| Canal | Joins | Opened |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Suez Canal** | Mediterranean Sea and Red Sea (Egypt) | 1869 |
| **Panama Canal** | Atlantic and Pacific Oceans (Panama) | 1914 |
| **Kiel Canal** | North Sea and Baltic Sea (Germany) | 1895 |
| **Corinth Canal** | Gulf of Corinth and Aegean Sea (Greece) | 1893 |

- The Suez Canal cuts the sea route between Europe and Asia, as ships no
  longer need to sail round the Cape of Good Hope. It was built under the French
  engineer **Ferdinand de Lesseps**.
- The Panama Canal works by locks because the two oceans stand at different
  levels, with a lake in the middle.
- In India, the **Indira Gandhi Canal** (Rajasthan) is among the longest
  irrigation canals in the world, fed from the Sutlej and Beas waters through
  the Harike barrage.

## 4. International Boundaries and Lines

| Line | Between | Hook |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Radcliffe Line** | India and Pakistan (and Bangladesh's western edge) | Drawn by **Cyril Radcliffe** in 1947 at Partition |
| **McMahon Line** | India (Arunachal Pradesh) and China (Tibet) | **Henry McMahon**, Simla Conference 1914 |
| **Line of Actual Control (LAC)** | India and China | De facto line; no agreed map |
| **Line of Control (LoC)** | Indian and Pakistani parts of Jammu and Kashmir | Formalised under the **Simla Agreement of 1972** |
| **Durand Line** | Pakistan and Afghanistan | **Mortimer Durand**, 1893 |
| **17th Parallel** | North and South Vietnam (pre-1975) | Divided Vietnam |
| **38th Parallel** | North and South Korea | Divided Korea |
| **49th Parallel** | USA and Canada | Western section of the US-Canada boundary |
| **Maginot Line** | France and Germany | French fortification of the 1930s |
| **Oder-Neisse Line** | Germany and Poland | Post-1945 boundary |

### India's Land Neighbours

| Neighbour | Land border (km) | Indian states and UTs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bangladesh | 4,096.7 | West Bengal, Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram |
| China | 3,488 | Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Sikkim, Arunachal Pradesh |
| Pakistan | 3,323 | Gujarat, Rajasthan, Punjab, Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh |
| Nepal | 1,751 | Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, Sikkim |
| Myanmar | 1,643 | Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Manipur, Mizoram |
| Bhutan | 699 | Sikkim, West Bengal, Assam, Arunachal Pradesh |
| Afghanistan | 106 | Ladakh (via Pakistan-occupied territory, as India claims) |

- The total land boundary is about **15,106.7 km**, and India has seven land
  neighbours. **Bangladesh** gives the longest border and **Afghanistan** the
  shortest.
- **West Bengal** is the only state touching Bangladesh, Nepal and Bhutan.
- The **India-Bangladesh Land Boundary Agreement** took effect in 2015 and
  exchanged enclaves between the two countries.
- **Sri Lanka and the Maldives** are sea neighbours, not land neighbours.

[example]
Question: "Which strait separates the Andaman and Nicobar groups?" Count the
degree in the name: the Ten Degree Channel lies near 10 degrees north and
splits the two groups. For Minicoy and the Maldives it is the Eight Degree
Channel instead.
[/example]

[summary]
Zoji La joins Srinagar and Leh, Rohtang links Kullu with Lahaul, Nathu La and
Jelep La lead from Sikkim to Tibet, Shipki La lies in Himachal, and the Palghat
Gap links Kerala and Tamil Nadu. The Palk Strait separates India and Sri Lanka,
the Ten Degree Channel parts Andaman from Nicobar, and the Eight Degree
Channel parts Minicoy from the Maldives. Suez (1869) and Panama (1914) are the
great canals. The Radcliffe, McMahon and Durand lines divide India-Pakistan,
India-China and Pakistan-Afghanistan, and India shares about 15,106.7 km of
land boundary with seven neighbours, longest with Bangladesh.
[/summary]
